    "289": {
      "es": "Note: *bored* and *boring* are same word!\n*Estoy aburrido* (I am bored)\n*Soy aburrido* (I am boring)"
    },
    "290": {
      "es": "The mind is *preoccupied* with (presumably) worrying thoughts"
    },
    "291": {
      "es": "From Latin *in* (inwards) and *odium* (hatred) from which *odious* derives (extremely unpleasant)"
    },
    "295": {
      "es": "Similar to *vent*"
    },
    "296": {
      "es": "Note *dormitorio* cannot be used since it specifically means *bedroom*"
    },
    "297": {
      "es": "Similar to *quatro*; paintings typically have four sides\nSimilar to *pintor* (painter)\nAlmost sounds similar to *Ace Ventura*"
    },
    "299": {
      "es": "*Suelo* has same root as *soil* and *sole*"
    },
    "300": {
      "es": "Same root as *speculate* (originally meaning *observe* or *view*)\nThinking of shaving (and a male) for masculine mnemonic"
    },
    "301": {
      "es": "Same root as *vase* (vessel)"
    },
    "303": {
      "es": "Same root as *tonsure* (ritualistic shaving of head)\nTo remember gender: *las tijeras de la tia* (Grok)"
    },
    "304": {
      "es": "For some reason reminds me of *cesspit*"
    },
    "305": {
      "es": "Same root as *compare*; in a sense, shopping involves lots of comparisons"
    },
    "306": {
      "es": "*Judias* supposedly derives from the fact that Jewish people used to eat such beans"
    },
    "310": {
      "es": "Sounds like *polla*"
    },
    "311": {
      "es": "Prefer *pimiento* which is more common than *morron*\n*Pimiento* is a specific type of pepper in English; the word is general in Spanish\n*La pimienta* refers to the spice (black pepper)"
    },
    "312": {
      "es": "Mnemonic (Grok): zany orange"
    },
    "313": {
      "es": "Prefer *repollo*\nSimilar to *pollo* (chicken)\nBoth words share same Latin origin *pullus* (young animal or sprout)"
    },
    "317": {
      "es": "(Not the same root) but linked to *vetch* (a family of legume plants)"
    },
    "318": {
      "es": "Remember: words ending in *-umbre* tend to be feminine"
    },
    "319": {
      "es": "Sounds like *acetic* (but these words have different roots)"
    },
    "320": {
      "es": "Same root as *ovary* and *ovulate*"
    },
    "321": {
      "es": "(Grok) feminine because a shopping list is something a lady makes"
    },
    "322": {
      "es": "Prefer *camarero/a*"
    },
    "323": {
      "es": "Prefer *boligrafo*\nConceptually decompose into *bola* (ball) and *grafo* (graph)"
    },
    "324": {
      "es": "From Latin *lapis* (stone) referring to usage of mineral or chalk historically to write\nLinked to English *lapis lazuli* gemstone"
    },
    "328": {
      "es": "Prefer *salario*"
    },
    "329": {
      "es": "Prefer *barco*\nSame root as English *embark* (originally meant to board a ship)"
    },
    "330": {
      "es": "This literally means *physical shape/condition*\ne.g. *Esta en buen estado fisico*\nFor fitness has a hobby, use *fitness*\ne.g. *Me gusta el fitness*"
    },
    "331": {
      "es": "Works for both studies and physical exercises:\n*Los ejercicios de espanol*\n*Me gusta hacer ejercicio*\n*Ahora voy a hacer mis ejercicios*"
    },
    "336": {
      "es": "Chevrolet *El Camino*\nBreaking Bad follow-up movie *El Camino* focused on Jesse Pinkman."
    },
    "337": {
      "es": "Prefer *acera* (meaning *sidewalk* or *pavement*)\nSame root as *face* (*facing* side of street)\nLike *hacer-a*"
    },
    "339": {
      "es": "Like *zoological*"
    },
    "340": {
      "es": "Prefer *cerro*\nFrom Latin *cirrus* (curl, lock of hair)\nSame root as *cirrus* clouds\nLike *cero* (zero)"
    },
    "341": {
      "es": "Prefer *verdura*\nLike *Ace Ventura*"
    },
    "343": {
      "es": "e.g. *Como verduras cada dia*\nRhymes with *nada* which has a slightly opposing meaning"
    },
    "345": {
      "es": "Note: this means ordinary (casual) and workplace meetings, not like \"reunion\" in English"
    },
    "350": {
      "es": "e.g. *El uso del mobil esta prohibido*\nAlso sounds like the Greek drink"
    },
    "352": {
      "es": "e.g. *Llandudno esta lejos*\nFrom Latin *laxus* (wide, loose) from which *relax* derives\n*Lejos* sounds similar to loose"
    },
    "354": {
      "es": "*Linda* means pretty, cute\n*Hermosa* means beautiful"
    },
    "355": {
      "es": "Grok mnemonic: *feo* looks like *fail* + *o* (fails the beauty test)"
    },
    "366": {
      "es": "Do not confuse with *el nombre* (name)"
    },
    "368": {
      "es": "Same root as *large* but different meaning"
    },
    "369": {
      "es": "e.g. *El bano esta abajo* (the bathroom is downstairs)\nNote: usage of estar for where something or someone is, even if they never move\nExactly like *trabajo* but without the *tr* at the start"
    },
    "370": {
      "es": "Prioritize *parte* which can mean almost any kind of part (physical or abstract) "
    },
    "371": {
      "es": "Prefer *terminado*"
    },
    "373": {
      "es": "Prefer *fuerte*\nDo not confuse with *la fuerza* (strength)"
    },
    "374": {
      "es": "Use *silencioso* for an environment: *mi habitacion es muy silenciosa*\nUse *callado* for a person: *esta chica esta bastante callada*\nGrok mnemonic: *callado* - *call* them and they go quiet\nAlso sounds a bit like Lamborghini *Gallardo*"
    },
    "376": {
      "es": "*Solamente* is slightly more emphatic\nVarious usages:\n*Este bolso cuesta solo veinte euros*\n*Esta nina tiene solamente cuatro anos*\n*Puedes completar el examen solo con boligrafo negro*"
    },
    "378": {
      "es": "Same root as *local* (pertaining to a place)\nAlso: spelled like *sugar*"
    },
    "379": {
      "es": "*Mayoria* sounds like *mayor*\nUse *mayoria* for most a group:\n*La mayoria de gente...* (most people...)\nUse *mas* for superlative most:\n*Lo mas importante es...* (the most important thing is...)"
    },
    "380": {
      "es": "*Oracion* means sentence while *frase* means phrase\nSame root as *oration*"
    },
    "381": {
      "es": "(Repeat of *grande* before)"
    },
    "382": {
      "es": "*Ayudar* sounds a bit like *I aid you* (Grok mnemonic)\nAlso sounds like *a Yoda* (personal mnemonic)\n*Necesito ayuda*\n*Me puedes ayudar?* (informal)\n*Te ayudo?*"
    },
    "384": {
      "es": "Same root as *direct*"
    },
    "385": {
      "es": "Sounds like *scared* (Arrested Development)"
    },
    "387": {
      "es": "Prefer *mezquino*\nSounds like *Ms Queen-o* (personal mnemonic)"
    },
    "388": {
      "es": "*Cualquier* means *whichever* (from *cual* and *quiera*)\ne.g. *Cualquier libro esta bien*\nUse *cualquier* for \"it doesn't matter which one\"\nUse *algun* or *alguno* when asking if something exists:\n*Hay alguien ahi?* (is anyone there?)\n*Tienes algo de comida?*"
    },
    "389": {
      "es": "Prefer *mismo*:\n*Tenemos las misma camisa*\n*Esta es la misma comida de ayer*\nAlmost sounds like *mismatch* which ironically means the opposite\n"
    },
    "394": {
      "es": "Prefer *fin*:\n*Esta es el fin del tour*\nNote: using *ser* (not *estar*) because it's about *what it is*, not a temporary state"
    },
    "395": {
      "es": "e.g. *tomate otra bebida* (have another drink)"
    },
    "397": {
      "es": "Like *terra*"
    },
    "400": {
      "es": "e.g. *El hombre viejo es muy amable*\nSame root as *amiable* "
    },
    "401": {
      "es": "Prefer *otra vez* (for now) although *de nuevo* is also common.\n*Otra vez* literally means *another time* (*vez* means time as in occasion or turn)\n*Dilo otra vez* (say that again)\n*Puedes hacerlo otra vez?* (can you do that again?)"
    },
    "406": {
      "es": "Prefer *feura* which means *not here right now*\n*Lejos* means *far away*\n*Mi mujer esta feura* (my wife is away)\nSounds a bit like *fuerza* (strength) without the *z*"
    },
    "408": {
      "es": "Same as word for *menu*"
    },
    "410": {
      "es": "From the verb *responder* (to reply)\n*Cual es la respuesta correcta?*"
    },
    "412": {
      "es": "Prefer *incorrecto*"
    },
    "414": {
      "es": "Can mean both *pastor* and *shepherd*"
    },
    "419": {
      "es": "Decomposes into *el pelo* (hair) plus *quero* (want); although this break-down is not accurate!"
    },
    "426": {
      "es": "Almost like *veteran* plus *-ario*"
    },
    "430": {
      "es": "(Grok) sounds like *as a fatter* (a fat flight attendant, perhaps)"
    },
    "436": {
      "es": "Like *ballerina*"
    },
    "437": {
      "es": "Similar to *el periodico* (newspaper)\nNote a *periodical* in English refers to a regularly published newspaper or magazine"
    },
    "439": {
      "es": "(Grok) sounds a bit like *guarantee*; a manager guarantees things get done"
    },
    "440": {
      "es": "Note: same as *la musica* (music) "
    },
    "445": {
      "es": "Like *costa del sol*"
    },
    "448": {
      "es": "Prefer *la medicina*"
    },
    "449": {
      "es": "Same prefix as *enfermera* (nurse)"
    },
    "450": {
      "es": "Same prefix as *enfermera* (nurse)"
    },
    "451": {
      "es": "*El dolor* means pain:\n*Dolor* sounds like *dollar* and pain costs you (Grok)\n*Garganta* means throat (same sound you make when you *gargle*)"
    },
    "452": {
      "es": "When you cough you might *toss* your head (Grok)\n*Tengo tos* (I have a cough)"
    },
    "458": {
      "es": "Formed by *jugar* plus *-ete* (meaning *little play*)"
    },
    "459": {
      "es": "Like *obscure*"
    },
    "460": {
      "es": "*Puedo ver una luza*\n*Azul claro*\n*Esta claro* (it's clear)\n*Claro que si* (yes, of course)"
    },
    "461": {
      "es": "e.g. *ciudad de Mexico*"
    },
    "462": {
      "es": "*Pueblo* has same root as *people*\nA town is a place where people live (Grok)"
    },
    "464": {
      "es": "From the French *pays* (country)"
    },
    "466": {
      "es": "Same word for *history*\n*Esa es una historia divertida*"
    },
    "467": {
      "es": "Do not confuse with *el plato* (plate) or *la plata* (money, silver)"
    },
    "470": {
      "es": "Do not confuse with *el puerto* (port)\nAlthough a door is like a *port* to another room"
    },
    "471": {
      "es": "Use *calido* when describing environment and people:\n*Ella as muy calida*\n*Esta colido* (it is hot [environment])\n*La comida no es caliente*\n*Calido* has same root as *calorie* (unit of heat energy)"
    },
    "472": {
      "es": "*Esta frio afuera* (it's cold outside)\n*Esta chica es bastante fria* (this girl is slightly cold)"
    },
    "479": {
      "es": "(Grok) think about the word *regal* and imagine unwrapping such a gift box"
    },
    "483": {
      "es": "Rhymes with *juego* (game)"
    },
    "484": {
      "es": "Sounds like *hail-o* (hail is essentially ice from the sky)"
    },
    "485": {
      "es": "Same root as *join* and *junction*, sharing the same prefix *jun-*"
    },
    "495": {
      "es": "Prefer *cara*\nSimilar sounding to *character*, which is almost like someone's inner *face*"
    },
    "496": {
      "es": "Same root as *quasi* in English"
    },
    "497": {
      "es": "Same root as *juvenile*"
    },
    "503": {
      "es": "Prefer *porcion*"
    },
    "504": {
      "es": "Same root as *secure*"
    },
    "508": {
      "es": "Well known brand of beer"
    },
    "514": {
      "es": "Same root as *edifice* (large building)"
    },
    "515": {
      "es": "The first part of the word sounds very similar to *ciudad* (city); be careful in the city!"
    },
    "522": {
      "es": "Prefer *lengua*"
    },
    "523": {
      "es": "Sounds like *avocado*"
    },
    "524": {
      "es": "Sounds like *counter*"
    },
    "527": {
      "es": "Sounds like someone who *deports* others?"
    },
    "531": {
      "es": "Repeat of previous"
    },
    "540": {
      "es": "Reminds me of *la rue* in French (street), although the words are not linked\nThe wheel rolls down the street"
    },
    "543": {
      "es": "Sounds similar to *rotund*"
    },
    "548": {
      "es": "Used in nuts *los frutos secos* (dry fruits)"
    },
    "549": {
      "es": "Sounds like *mojito* (which is a drink, so must be wet)"
    },
    "550": {
      "es": "Sounds similar to *pescado* (fish); lots of fish are heavy (tuna, sharks)"
    },
    "551": {
      "es": "Prefer *ligero* (sounds like *liger*, a giant beast, the opposite of light-weight!)"
    }
